00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:07,262
Chapter 8: Teratology, Teratogens, and Fetotoxic.

3
00:00:07,262 --> 00:00:09,600
Agents Teratology.

4
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:13,162
Criteria For Determining Teratogenicity.

5
00:00:13,162 --> 00:00:15,562
Studies In Pregnant Women.

6
00:00:15,562 --> 00:00:18,500
Counseling For Medication Exposure.

7
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:20,775
Labeling Requirements.

8
00:00:20,775 --> 00:00:24,012
Teratogenic And Fetotoxic Agents.

9
00:00:24,012 --> 00:00:25,912
Drugs Of Abuse.

10
00:00:25,912 --> 00:00:27,675
References.

11
00:00:27,675 --> 00:00:30,037
Birth defects are common.

12
00:00:30,037 --> 00:00:40,125
Of all newborns, 2 to 3 percent have a major congenital abnormality detectable at birth (Cragan, 2009; Dolk, 2010).

13
00:00:40,125 --> 00:00:46,237
Some medications undoubtedly pose significant risk to the developing embryo or fetus .

14
00:00:46,237 --> 00:00:58,412
However, 80 percent of birth defects do not have an obvious etiology, and of those with an identified cause, nearly 95 percent of cases have chromosomal or genetic origins .

15
00:00:58,412 --> 00:01:08,262
The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) (2018) estimates that <1 percent of all birth defects are caused by medications .

16
00:01:08,262 --> 00:01:10,437
Table 8-1.

17
00:01:10,437 --> 00:01:15,450
Selected Teratogenic and Fetotoxic Agents Figure 8-1.

18
00:01:15,450 --> 00:01:18,212
Etiology of birth defects.

19
00:01:18,212 --> 00:01:28,137
Known and unknown causes of 5504 birth defects from a population-based review of 270,878 births.

20
00:01:28,137 --> 00:01:33,400
That said, significant concern surrounds medication use in pregnancy.

21
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:40,087
This is because many pregnant women are prescribed medications and because safety data are often lacking.

22
00:01:40,087 --> 00:01:51,750
Investigators from the National Birth Defects Prevention Study found that women take an average of two to three medications per pregnancy and that 70 percent use medication in the first trimester .

23
00:01:51,750 --> 00:02:00,250
A population-based review from Canada found that 65 percent of women fill ≥1 prescription during pregnancy .

24
00:02:00,250 --> 00:02:10,425
Between 2010 and 2019, the FDA approved 290 new drugs, and only 11 percent had human data related to pregnancy .

25
00:02:10,425 --> 00:02:12,312
Teratology.

26
00:02:12,312 --> 00:02:20,037
The study of birth defects and their etiology is termed teratology, derived from the Greek teratos, meaning monster.

27
00:02:20,037 --> 00:02:29,450
A teratogen may be broadly defined as any agent that acts during embryonic or fetal development to produce a permanent alteration of form or function.

28
00:02:29,450 --> 00:02:44,625
Thus, a teratogen may be a medication or other chemical substance, a physical or environmental factor such as heat or radiation, a maternal metabolite as in diabetes or phenylketonuria, or an infection such as cytomegalovirus.

29
00:02:44,625 --> 00:02:49,550
Strictly defined, a teratogen causes structural abnormalities.

30
00:02:49,550 --> 00:02:56,737
A hadegen —after the god Hades—is an agent that interferes with normal maturation and function of an organ.

31
00:02:56,737 --> 00:03:00,050
A trophogen is an agent that alters growth.

32
00:03:00,050 --> 00:03:08,975
Substances in the latter two groups typically affect development in the fetal period or postnatally and are thus fetotoxins rather than teratogens.

33
00:03:08,975 --> 00:03:12,975
# Criteria for Determining Teratogenicity.

34
00:03:12,975 --> 00:03:23,137
The guidelines shown in Table 8-2 were proposed by Shepard (1994) as a framework for discussion and have proved useful for nearly 30 years.

35
00:03:23,137 --> 00:03:32,212
Although each individual criterion is not required to establish teratogenicity, the following tenets should be considered : Table 8-2.

36
00:03:32,212 --> 00:03:38,487
Criteria for Determining Teratogenicity • The abnormality has been completely characterized.

37
00:03:38,487 --> 00:03:47,137
This is preferably done by a geneticist or dysmorphologist because different genetic and environmental factors may produce similar defects.

38
00:03:47,137 --> 00:03:57,987
It is easiest to prove causation when a rare exposure produces a rare anomaly, when at least three cases with the same exposure have been identified, and when the defect is severe.

39
00:03:57,987 --> 00:04:00,875
The agent must cross the placenta.

40
00:04:00,875 --> 00:04:14,250
Although almost all drugs cross the placenta, transport must be of sufficient quantity to directly influence embryonic or fetal development or to alter maternal or placental metabolism to exert an indirect effect.

41
00:04:14,250 --> 00:04:32,212
Placental transfer depends on maternal metabolism; on specific characteristics of the drug, such as protein binding and storage, molecular size, electrical charge, and lipid solubility; and on placental metabolism, such as by the cytochrome P450 enzyme system.

42
00:04:32,212 --> 00:04:38,512
In early pregnancy, the placenta also has a relatively thick membrane that slows diffusion.

43
00:04:38,512 --> 00:04:42,625
Exposure must occur during a critical developmental period.

44
00:04:42,625 --> 00:04:50,337
The preimplantation period is the 2 weeks between fertilization and implantation and is known as the “all or none” period.

45
00:04:50,337 --> 00:04:56,250
As the zygote undergoes cleavage, an insult damaging a large number of cells typically

46
00:04:56,404 --> 00:04:58,854
causes embryonic death.

47
00:04:58,804 --> 00:05:05,666
However, if only a few cells are injured, compensation may be possible and allow normal development.

48
00:05:05,666 --> 00:05:10,866
The embryonic period extends from the second through the eighth week postconception.

49
00:05:10,866 --> 00:05:17,654
It encompasses organogenesis and is thus the most crucial period with regard to structural malformations.

50
00:05:17,654 --> 00:05:25,304
Critical developmental periods for each organ system are illustrated in Figure 7-2 (p. 122).

51
00:05:25,304 --> 00:05:33,841
The fetal period, which is beyond 8 weeks postconception, is characterized by continued maturation and functional development.

52
00:05:33,841 --> 00:05:37,741
During this time, certain organs remain vulnerable.

53
00:05:37,741 --> 00:05:41,554
A biologically plausible association is supportive.

54
00:05:41,554 --> 00:05:48,779
Because birth defects and medication exposures are both common, they may be temporally but not causally related.

55
00:05:48,779 --> 00:05:52,579
Epidemiological findings must be consistent.

56
00:05:52,579 --> 00:06:04,516
Because initial evaluation of teratogen exposure is often retrospective, it may be hampered by recall bias, inadequate reporting, and incomplete assessment of the exposed population.

57
00:06:04,516 --> 00:06:12,429
Potential confounding factors include varying dosages, concomitant drug therapy, and comorbid maternal disease(s).

58
00:06:12,429 --> 00:06:18,316
Familial and environmental variables also can influence development of birth defects.

59
00:06:18,316 --> 00:06:27,054
Thus, an important criterion for teratogenicity is that two or more highquality epidemiological studies report similar findings.

60
00:06:27,054 --> 00:06:37,604
Last, a relative risk of 3.0 or greater is generally considered necessary to support the hypothesis, whereas a lesser risk is interpreted with caution .

61
00:06:37,604 --> 00:06:42,154
The suspected teratogen causes a defect in animal studies.

62
00:06:42,154 --> 00:06:53,166
This criterion is not obligatory, and for litigation purposes, establishment of a causal relationship between an exposure and an outcome in humans requires human data .

63
00:06:53,166 --> 00:07:01,666
Failure to employ these tenets and criteria has contributed to erroneous conclusions regarding the safety of some widely used drugs.

64
00:07:01,666 --> 00:07:06,654
The poster child for this is the medicolegal fiasco surrounding Bendectin.

65
00:07:06,654 --> 00:07:13,366
This antiemetic was a combination of doxylamine and pyridoxine, with or without dicyclomine.

66
00:07:13,366 --> 00:07:21,454
More than 30 million women used this drug worldwide, and it was safe and effective for nausea and vomiting in early pregnancy.

67
00:07:21,454 --> 00:07:28,129
The 3-percent congenital anomaly rate among exposed fetuses was not different from the background rate .

68
00:07:28,129 --> 00:07:43,566
Despite considerable evidence that this combination of an antihistamine and a B-vitamin is not teratogenic, Bendectin was the target of numerous lawsuits, and the financial burden of defending these forced its withdrawal from the marketplace in 1983.

69
00:07:43,566 --> 00:07:48,279
Consequently, hospitalization rates for hyperemesis doubled .

70
00:07:48,279 --> 00:07:58,804
Ironically, the combination of doxylamine and pyridoxine was subsequently remarketed under the brand name Diclegis and was approved by the FDA in 2013.

71
00:07:58,804 --> 00:08:01,729
# Studies in Pregnant Women.

72
00:08:01,729 --> 00:08:08,529
The study of medication safety—or teratogenicity—in pregnant women is fraught with complications.

73
00:08:08,529 --> 00:08:12,666
First, animal studies are necessary but insufficient.

74
00:08:12,666 --> 00:08:25,104
For example, thalidomide was considered harmless in several animal species but resulted in phocomelia in thousands of children born across Europe in the late 1950s and early 1960s.

75
00:08:25,104 --> 00:08:31,529
Second, medications are rarely approved by the FDA for a pregnancy-related indication.

76
00:08:31,529 --> 00:08:44,716
Despite the Common Rule’s removal of pregnant women as a vulnerable population, pregnant women are routinely excluded from research (Health and Human Services, 2017; Spong, 2018).

77
00:08:44,716 --> 00:08:51,404
Last, drug concentration and thus embryo-fetal exposure are affected by pregnancy physiology.

78
00:08:51,404 --> 00:09:00,904
This includes changes in volume of distribution, cardiac output, gastrointestinal absorption, hepatic metabolism, and renal clearance.

79
00:09:00,904 --> 00:09:11,016
In the absence of research trials, counseling is based on case reports or series, case-control studies, cohort studies, and pregnancy registry data.

80
00:09:11,016 --> 00:09:19,841
Case Reports and Series Many major teratogens were first described by clinicians who observed a rare defect occurring after a rare exposure.

81
00:09:19,841 --> 00:09:23,179
This has been termed the “astute clinician model” .

82
00:09:23,179 --> 00:09:34,654
Congenital rubella syndrome was identified in this way by Gregg (1941), an Australian ophthalmologist whose observations challenged the view that the uterine environment was

83
00:09:34,804 --> 00:09:37,591
impervious to noxious agents.

84
00:09:37,541 --> 00:09:46,916
Other teratogens identified through case series include thalidomide and alcohol (Jones, 1973; Lenz, 1962).

85
00:09:46,916 --> 00:09:58,204
Shepard (2002a) recommended that establishment of teratogenicity in this way requires proven exposure at a critical time in development and at least three such cases.

86
00:09:58,204 --> 00:10:11,729
Unfortunately, teratogens are less likely to be identified if the exposure is uncommon, if the defects are relatively nonspecific, or if abnormalities develop in only a small proportion of exposed fetuses.

87
00:10:11,729 --> 00:10:16,104
A major limitation of case series is their lack of a control group.

88
00:10:16,104 --> 00:10:28,654
Case-control Studies These studies begin with groups of affected infants (cases) and unaffected controls and are structured to allow retrospective assessment of prenatal exposure to particular substances.

89
00:10:28,654 --> 00:10:33,104
Case-control studies are an efficient way to study rare outcomes .

90
00:10:33,104 --> 00:10:39,279
These permit investigators to evaluate associations and generate useful hypotheses.

91
00:10:39,279 --> 00:10:44,441
However, case-control studies have inherent potential for recall bias.

92
00:10:44,441 --> 00:10:51,779
Namely, parents of an affected infant are often more likely to recall exposure than those whose child is not ill.

93
00:10:51,779 --> 00:10:59,854
Confounding by indication is another concern, that is, the indication for the medication may be the cause of the birth defect.

94
00:10:59,854 --> 00:11:07,916
And importantly, birth defect registries have statistical power to detect small differences that may not be clinically meaningful.

95
00:11:07,916 --> 00:11:18,891
Grimes and Schulz (2012) have cautioned that unless odds ratios in case-control studies are above three- to fourfold, the observed associations may not be correct.

96
00:11:18,891 --> 00:11:22,291
The National Birth Defects Prevention Study.

97
00:11:22,291 --> 00:11:39,954
Funded by Congress and coordinated by the National Center on Birth Defects and Developmental Disabilities, the National Birth Defects Prevention Study (NBDPS) took place between 1997 and 2013 across ten states with active birth defects surveillance programs.

98
00:11:39,954 --> 00:11:44,654
It is an excellent example of a population-based casecontrol study.

99
00:11:44,654 --> 00:11:50,766
The study involved approximately 32,000 cases and nearly 12,000 controls.

100
00:11:50,766 --> 00:11:55,741
Live births, stillbirths, and terminated pregnancies were included.

101
00:11:55,741 --> 00:12:13,266
Clinical geneticists reviewed each potential case, and standardized telephone interviews were conducted with women whose pregnancies were affected or unaffected to obtain information regarding medication exposure and risk factors (Mitchell, 2011; Reefhuis, 2015).

102
00:12:13,266 --> 00:12:22,854
The NBDPS identified novel—although often small—associations between individual birth defects and several classes of medications.

103
00:12:22,854 --> 00:12:46,404
These include antibiotics, antidepressants, antiemetics, antihypertensives, asthma medications, nonsteroidal antiinflammatory drugs (NSAIDs), and opioids (Ailes, 2016; Broussard, 2011; Fisher, 2018; Hernandez, 2012; Lin, 2012; Munsie, 2011).

104
00:12:46,404 --> 00:12:53,566
Pregestational diabetes was significantly associated with 46 of 50 different abnormalities studied .

105
00:12:53,566 --> 00:13:12,179
The NBDPS also found associations between birth defects and exposures such as secondhand smoke, pesticides, and nitrogen oxide, which is a marker of traffic-related air pollution (Hoyt, 2016; Rocheleau, 2015; Stingone, 2017).

106
00:13:12,179 --> 00:13:17,266
The NBDPS had several key limitations related to study design.

107
00:13:17,266 --> 00:13:25,016
First, interviews were conducted 6 weeks to 2 years following delivery, which raised the likelihood of recall bias.

108
00:13:25,016 --> 00:13:31,141
For example, 25 percent of women could not remember which antibiotic they had taken .

109
00:13:31,141 --> 00:13:41,041
Additionally, only two thirds of women agreed to participate, and there were differences in ethnicity and socioeconomic status between cases and controls.

110
00:13:41,041 --> 00:13:44,966
These factors may have contributed to selection bias .

111
00:13:44,966 --> 00:13:52,716
Further, medical records were not reviewed to verify dosage, and this precluded assessment of dose-response relationships.

112
00:13:52,716 --> 00:14:06,979
Last, a major limitation was that because the NBDPS included only a small number of cases of each birth defect and analyzed them for many different maternal exposures, it was not possible to adjust for multiple comparisons.

113
00:14:06,979 --> 00:14:12,091
As a result, some of the observed associations were likely due to chance .

114
00:14:12,091 --> 00:14:23,641
As one example, the study of antibiotics and birth defects included 43 comparisons and identified four significant associations, but chance alone predicted that two

115
00:14:23,812 --> 00:14:26,687
associations would be identified .

116
00:14:26,637 --> 00:14:35,274
Importantly, the NBDPS was able to identify statistically significant odds ratios for which the absolute risk was quite low.

117
00:14:35,274 --> 00:14:40,599
Such findings have the potential to complicate counseling and prenatal management.

118
00:14:40,599 --> 00:14:48,774
In many instances, the risk identified by the NBDPS was as low as 1 case per 1000 exposed pregnancies.

119
00:14:48,774 --> 00:14:56,587
Cohort Studies These studies begin with cohorts of pregnant women who are exposed or unexposed to a particular medication.

120
00:14:56,587 --> 00:15:02,499
The percentage of infants or children affected with birth defects is examined in each cohort.

121
00:15:02,499 --> 00:15:09,112
Because individual birth defects are rare, cohort studies require a very large sample size.

122
00:15:09,112 --> 00:15:17,974
Medicaid datasets and private insurance claims databases are commonly used for cohort studies of teratogenicity in the United States .

123
00:15:17,974 --> 00:15:27,462
Inability to adjust for confounding variables—such as the indication for which the medication was needed—is an important limitation of this study design.

124
00:15:27,462 --> 00:15:36,137
Pregnancy Registries Potentially harmful agents may be monitored by clinicians who prospectively enroll exposed pregnancies in a registry.

125
00:15:36,137 --> 00:15:42,874
The FDA (2021) maintains an active webpage of Pregnancy Exposure Registries.

126
00:15:42,874 --> 00:16:14,224
As of 2021, this included registries for 115 individual medications and for medication groups used to treat asthma, attention deficit hyperactivity disorder, autoimmune diseases, cancer, diabetes, epilepsy, hepatitis B and C, human immunodeficiency virus infection, hypercholesterolemia, inborn errors of metabolism, inflammatory bowel disease, multiple sclerosis, narcolepsy, psychiatric illness, and transplant rejection.

127
00:16:14,224 --> 00:16:19,949
Similar to case series, exposure registries are hampered by lack of a control group.

128
00:16:19,949 --> 00:16:28,437
The prevalence of an abnormality identified through a registry requires knowledge of the baseline prevalence of that anomaly in the population.

129
00:16:28,437 --> 00:16:34,262
Investigators typically use a birth defect registry to assess population prevalence.

130
00:16:34,262 --> 00:16:45,824
One example is the Metropolitan Atlanta Congenital Defects Program, which is an active surveillance program established in 1967 for fetuses and infants with birth defects.

131
00:16:45,824 --> 00:16:48,762
Counseling For Medication Exposure.

132
00:16:48,762 --> 00:16:55,687
Questions regarding medication and illicit drug use should be part of routine preconceptional and prenatal care.

133
00:16:55,687 --> 00:16:58,124
Misinformation is common.

134
00:16:58,124 --> 00:17:08,462
Individuals tend to underestimate the background risk for birth defects in the general population and exaggerate potential risks associated with medication exposure.

135
00:17:08,462 --> 00:17:24,262
In one population-based study of more than 270,000 births from Utah that included 5500 fetuses and infants with major birth defects, only 4 cases were attributed to medication exposure (see Fig. 8-1) .

136
00:17:24,262 --> 00:17:35,174
And yet, Koren and colleagues (1989) reported that a fourth of women exposed to nonteratogenic drugs thought they had a 25-percent risk for fetal anomalies.

137
00:17:35,174 --> 00:17:40,237
Misinformation may be amplified by inaccurate reports in the lay press.

138
00:17:40,237 --> 00:17:46,649
Knowledgeable counseling may allay anxiety considerably and may even avert pregnancy termination.

139
00:17:46,649 --> 00:17:52,787
Several sources are available to assist providers with accurate and updated risk information.

140
00:17:52,787 --> 00:18:02,274
With recent changes to the FDA labeling requirements, discussed next, the manufacturer’s prescribing information has become increasingly helpful.

141
00:18:02,274 --> 00:18:06,212
We recommend using this content for initial counseling.

142
00:18:06,212 --> 00:18:14,724
Published research studies can be identified using PubMed, a free database tool from the National Center for Biomedical Information.

143
00:18:14,724 --> 00:18:25,512
Additionally, online databases such as Reprotox, TERIS, and Shepard’s Online Catalog of Teratogenic Agents offer detailed reviews of medication risks.

144
00:18:25,512 --> 00:18:33,474
Lactmed, a database from the National Library of Medicine, specifically deals with medication use by breastfeeding women.

145
00:18:33,474 --> 00:18:40,049
Its entries on specific medications describe levels in breast milk and potential effects on the infant.

146
00:18:40,049 --> 00:18:45,637
Ultimately, it is the responsibility of the clinician to interpret risk information.

147
00:18:45,637 --> 00:18:55,849
Medication dosage and route, timing of exposure during pregnancy, other medications used, and underlying medical condition(s) are considered in this analysis.

148
00:18:55,849 --> 00:18:59,837
Last, the risk from lack of treatment also is weighed.

149
00:18:59,837 --> 00:19:02,649
# Labeling Requirements.

150
00:19:02,649 --> 00:19:05,999
In 1979, the FDA

151
00:19:06,148 --> 00:19:15,898
developed a letter classification system (A, B, C, D, X) to provide therapeutic guidance for prescribing medications in pregnancy.

152
00:19:15,848 --> 00:19:26,785
These letter categories were intended to simplify risk-benefit data using summary statements regarding available evidence from human or animal studies of embryonic–fetal risk.

153
00:19:26,785 --> 00:19:35,335
Unfortunately, information regarding medication risk was often incomplete and led to an overreliance on the category definition.

154
00:19:35,335 --> 00:19:45,798
To address these deficiencies, new FDA (2014, 2020b) labeling requirements were created and went into effect in 2015.

155
00:19:45,798 --> 00:19:52,685
The label provides a framework to aid prescribing decisions by more clearly communicating risks and benefits.

156
00:19:52,685 --> 00:20:01,098
Information about each medication now incorporates a detailed summary of risks, clinical considerations, and available data .

157
00:20:01,098 --> 00:20:04,698
Registry information is included when available.

158
00:20:04,698 --> 00:20:14,823
For each medication, a lactation subsection is provided, and a section addressing potential risks in females and males of reproductive potential is presented.

159
00:20:14,823 --> 00:20:25,885
In addition to risks associated with specific medications, the label is required to include the background risk of major birth defects and miscarriage in the general population.

160
00:20:25,885 --> 00:20:32,273
Because of the level of detail in the new labels, the information can be indispensable for counseling.

161
00:20:32,273 --> 00:20:34,448
Table 8-3.

162
00:20:34,448 --> 00:20:40,760
Food and Drug Administration Pregnancy Labeling Requirements # Presenting Risk Information.

163
00:20:40,760 --> 00:20:51,398
Counseling should cover not only the embryonic and fetal risks from drug exposure, but also the risks and/or genetic implications of the condition for which the drug is administered.

164
00:20:51,398 --> 00:20:56,035
Risks associated with not treating the condition also are described.

165
00:20:56,035 --> 00:21:00,535
Even the manner in which information is presented affects perception.

166
00:21:00,535 --> 00:21:15,673
For example, women given negative information—such as a 2-percent chance of a malformed newborn—are more likely to perceive an exaggerated risk than women given positive information —such as a 98-percent chance of an unaffected infant .

167
00:21:15,673 --> 00:21:28,910
Instead of citing a higher odds ratio, it may be helpful to provide the absolute risk for a particular defect or the attributable risk, which is the difference between prevalence in exposed and unexposed individuals .

168
00:21:28,910 --> 00:21:48,410
The association between oral corticosteroid medications and cleft lip sounds far more concerning when presented as a tripling or 200-percent increase in risk than when described as an increase from 1 to 3 cases per 1000 or as a 99.7-percent likelihood of no cleft development following exposure.

169
00:21:48,410 --> 00:21:56,635
With a few notable exceptions, most commonly prescribed drugs and medications can be used with relative safety during pregnancy.

170
00:21:56,635 --> 00:22:06,598
Many drugs discussed in this chapter are low-risk teratogens, which are medications that produce defects in fewer than 10 per 1000 maternal exposures .

171
00:22:06,598 --> 00:22:19,260
Because risks conferred by low-risk teratogens are so close to the population background rate of fetal anomalies, they may not be a major factor in deciding whether to discontinue treatment for an important condition .

172
00:22:19,260 --> 00:22:25,473
All women have an approximate 3-percent chance of having a fetus or newborn with a major anomaly.

173
00:22:25,473 --> 00:22:35,648
Although exposure to a confirmed teratogen may elevate this risk, the magnitude of the increase is usually only 1 or 2 percent or at most, doubled or tripled.

174
00:22:35,648 --> 00:22:40,385
The concept of risk versus benefit is often central to counseling.

175
00:22:40,385 --> 00:22:47,385
Some untreated diseases pose a more serious threat to both mother and fetus than medication exposure risks.

176
00:22:47,385 --> 00:22:50,623
Teratogenic And Fetotoxic Agents.

177
00:22:50,623 --> 00:23:02,073
Considering the thousands of compounds available, relatively few medications and other substances are considered to be major human teratogens or to have significant fetotoxicity.

178
00:23:02,073 --> 00:23:06,260
The most common examples are listed in Table 8-1.

179
00:23:06,260 --> 00:23:16,298
With few exceptions, in every clinical situation potentially requiring therapy with a known teratogen, alternative drugs can be given with relative safety.

180
00:23:16,298 --> 00:23:24,760
Realizing limitations in available evidence, pregnant women should be advised to take any medication only when it is clearly needed.

181
00:23:24,760 --> 00:23:32,723
Detailed sonography is generally indicated if the fetus has been exposed to any major teratogen during the embryonic period.

182
00:23:32,723 --> 00:23:34,860
# Alcohol.

183
00:23:34,860 --> 00:23:36,723
Ethanol is

184
00:23:36,892 --> 00:23:39,754
a potent and prevalent teratogen.

185
00:23:39,704 --> 00:23:45,154
It is considered the leading cause of preventable developmental disabilities worldwide .

186
00:23:45,154 --> 00:23:50,679
In the United States, alcohol use is reported by 10 percent of pregnant women .

187
00:23:50,679 --> 00:23:59,754
Nearly 10 percent of such women admit to binge drinking in the first trimester, and 1 percent report binge drinking in the second and third trimesters.

188
00:23:59,754 --> 00:24:04,992
The fetal effects of alcohol abuse have been recognized since the 1800s.

189
00:24:04,992 --> 00:24:16,817
Lemoine (1968) and Jones (1973) and their coworkers are credited with describing the spectrum of alcohol-related fetal defects known as fetal alcohol syndrome.

190
00:24:16,817 --> 00:24:20,379
Criteria are shown in Table 8-4 .

191
00:24:20,379 --> 00:24:27,329
For every child with the syndrome, many more are born with neurobehavioral deficits from alcohol exposure.

192
00:24:27,329 --> 00:24:52,254
Fetal alcohol spectrum disorder (FASD) is an umbrella term that includes five conditions attributed to prenatal alcohol damage: (1) fetal alcohol syndrome, (2) partial fetal alcohol syndrome, (3) alcohol-related birth defects, (4) alcohol-related neurodevelopmental disorder, and (5) neurobehavioral disorder associated with prenatal alcohol exposure .

193
00:24:52,254 --> 00:25:02,554
The prevalence of FASD exceeds 1 percent in 76 countries, and 1 in 13 women who consume alcohol in pregnancy has a child with FASD .

194
00:25:02,554 --> 00:25:14,117
The birth prevalence of fetal alcohol syndrome is estimated to be as high as 1 percent in the United States (Centers for Disease Control, 2015; Guerri, 2009).

195
00:25:14,117 --> 00:25:23,404
Further, studies of school-aged children have identified FASD in 2 to 5 percent (May, 2009, 2014).

196
00:25:23,404 --> 00:25:25,704
Table 8-4.

197
00:25:25,704 --> 00:25:36,667
Criteria for Prenatal Alcohol Exposure, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, and Alcohol-Related Birth Defects Criteria Fetal alcohol syndrome has specific criteria .

198
00:25:36,667 --> 00:25:47,404
These include central nervous system (CNS) abnormalities, pre- or postnatal growth impairment, and a characteristic pattern of minor facial abnormalities .

199
00:25:47,404 --> 00:25:53,117
Similar criteria have been established for the other conditions that make up FASD .

200
00:25:53,117 --> 00:25:58,742
Prenatal alcohol exposure criteria also are available to assist with assessment.

201
00:25:58,742 --> 00:26:00,892
Figure 8-2.

202
00:26:00,892 --> 00:26:03,304
Fetal alcohol syndrome.

203
00:26:03,304 --> 00:26:06,142
A. At 2½ years.

204
00:26:06,142 --> 00:26:08,479
B. At 12 years.

205
00:26:08,479 --> 00:26:17,479
Note persistence of short palpebral fissures, epicanthal folds, flat midface, hypoplastic philtrum, and thin upper vermilion border.

206
00:26:17,479 --> 00:26:23,542
Reproduced with permission from Streissguth AP, Clarren, SK, Jones KL.

207
00:26:23,542 --> 00:26:30,017
Natural history of fetal alcohol syndrome: a 10-year follow-up of eleven patients, Lancet.

208
00:26:30,017 --> 00:26:37,129
1985 Jul 13;2(8446):85–91.

209
00:26:37,129 --> 00:26:46,017
Alcohol-related birth defects include cardiac and renal anomalies, orthopedic problems, and abnormalities of the eyes and ears .

210
00:26:46,017 --> 00:26:53,554
An association has further been reported between periconceptional alcohol use and omphalocele and gastroschisis .

211
00:26:53,554 --> 00:26:59,967
There are no established sonographic criteria for prenatal diagnosis of fetal alcohol syndrome.

212
00:26:59,967 --> 00:27:08,592
At Parkland Hospital, we reserve detailed sonography for pregnancies that have met the exposure criteria listed in Table 8-4.

213
00:27:08,592 --> 00:27:13,092
Thirdtrimester assessment of fetal growth also should be considered.

214
00:27:13,092 --> 00:27:23,367
Fetal vulnerability to alcohol is modified by genetic and environmental factors, nutritional status, coexisting maternal disease, and maternal age .

215
00:27:23,367 --> 00:27:38,567
Binge drinking, however, is believed to pose particularly high risk for alcohol-related birth defects and has also been linked to a greater risk for stillbirth (Centers for Disease Control, 2015; Strandberg-Larsen, 2008).

216
00:27:38,567 --> 00:27:59,242
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), and the American Academy of Pediatrics have stressed that no amount of alcohol can be considered safe in pregnancy (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020a; Williams, 2015).

217
00:27:59,242 --> 00:28:02,504
# Antiepileptic Medications.

218
00:28:02,504 --> 00:28:10,829
Traditionally, women with epilepsy requiring treatment with medication were informed that their risk for fetal malformations was increased.

219
00:28:10,829 --> 00:28:17,854
More recent data suggest that the risk may not be as great as once thought, particularly for newer agents.

220
00:28:17,854 --> 00:28:25,504
The most frequently reported anomalies are orofacial clefts, cardiac malformations, and neural-tube defects.

221
00:28:25,504 --> 00:28:30,592
Of agents in current use, valproic acid confers the highest risk .

222
00:28:30,592 --> 00:28:37,004
The North American Antiepileptic Drug (NAAED) Pregnancy Registry reported that

223
00:28:37,156 --> 00:28:43,631
major malformations developed in 9 percent of fetuses with first-trimester valproate exposure.

224
00:28:43,581 --> 00:28:47,856
This included a 4-percent risk for neural-tube defects .

225
00:28:47,856 --> 00:29:06,306
School-aged children with prior in utero exposure to valproic acid have poorer cognitive development —including significantly lower intelligence quotient (IQ) scores—than children exposed to other antiepileptic drugs (Bromley, 2014; Meador, 2009).

226
00:29:06,306 --> 00:29:18,681
Regarding other specific anticonvulsants, one metaanalysis identified increased malformation rates among exposed children compared with rates among children born to women with untreated epilepsy.

227
00:29:18,681 --> 00:29:31,618
Rates were twofold higher among children exposed to carbamazepine or phenytoin, threefold higher among those exposed to phenobarbital, and fourfold higher among those exposed to topiramate as monotherapy .

228
00:29:31,618 --> 00:29:37,456
The risk for fetal malformations is approximately doubled if multiple agents are required .

229
00:29:37,456 --> 00:29:44,531
Several older anticonvulsants may produce a constellation of findings similar to the fetal hydantoin syndrome.

230
00:29:44,531 --> 00:29:58,306
The syndrome is characterized by distinctive features such as hypertelorism, low nasal bridge, and midface hypoplasia; hypoplasia of the distal phalanges and nails; growth impairment; and intellectual disability.

231
00:29:58,306 --> 00:30:10,793
Importantly, evidence to date suggests that these risks do not appear to hold for the newer agents levetiracetam and lamotrigine (Mølgaard-Nielsen, 2011; Weston, 2016).

232
00:30:10,793 --> 00:30:23,418
A review of 208 pregnancies with first-trimester lamotrigine exposure from the Israeli Teratology Information Service found no increase in the rate of major malformations and no case of oral cleft .

233
00:30:23,418 --> 00:30:35,806
Similarly, the International Lamotrigine Pregnancy Registry followed more than 1500 pregnancies with first trimester exposure and observed no effect on the rate of major malformations .

234
00:30:35,806 --> 00:30:47,368
The Motherisk Program reviewed eight studies of levetiracetam and concluded that monotherapy was associated with a 2-percent major malformation rate, no different from that for the general population .

235
00:30:47,368 --> 00:30:56,831
More recently, a review of 465 pregnancies reported to the Levetiracetam Pregnancy Registry found no evidence of teratogenicity .

236
00:30:56,831 --> 00:31:04,868
Providers are encouraged to enroll pregnant women treated with antiepileptic medication in the NAAED Pregnancy Registry.

237
00:31:04,868 --> 00:31:11,431
Management of epilepsy in pregnancy is discussed in Chapter 63 (p. 1128).

238
00:31:11,431 --> 00:31:16,193
# Angiotensin-converting Enzyme Inhibitors and Receptor.

239
00:31:16,193 --> 00:31:23,968
Blocking Drugs These medications may result in angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE)inhibitor fetopathy.

240
00:31:23,968 --> 00:31:28,818
Normal renal development depends on the fetal reninangiotensin system.

241
00:31:28,818 --> 00:31:40,806
ACE-inhibitor medication may cause fetal hypotension and renal hypoperfusion, with subsequent ischemia and anuria (Guron, 2000; Pryde, 1993).

242
00:31:40,806 --> 00:31:51,456
Reduced perfusion can result in fetal-growth restriction and calvarium maldevelopment, and oligohydramnios may lead to pulmonary hypoplasia and limb contractures .

243
00:31:51,456 --> 00:32:02,368
Because angiotensin-receptor blockers have a similar mechanism of action, concerns regarding fetotoxicity have been generalized to include this entire medication class.

244
00:32:02,368 --> 00:32:17,943
A recent review of nearly 200 pregnancies from teratology information services found a 30-percent fetopathy risk with angiotensin-receptor blockers compared with a 3-percent risk with ACE-inhibitor exposure beyond 20 weeks’ gestation .

245
00:32:17,943 --> 00:32:23,456
Concerns about ACE-inhibitor embryotoxicity have largely been disproven.

246
00:32:23,456 --> 00:32:39,531
In 2006, a review of 29,000 infants from the Tennessee Medicaid database identified a two- to threefold greater risk for neonatal cardiac and CNS abnormalities among the 209 that had prenatal ACE-inhibitor exposure .

247
00:32:39,531 --> 00:32:44,231
Subsequent larger studies have not corroborated these observations.

248
00:32:44,231 --> 00:32:55,981
In a retrospective cohort study of more than 460,000 pregnancies, risks for birth defects were not higher with ACE inhibitors than with other antihypertensive medications .

249
00:32:55,981 --> 00:33:10,218
Similarly, a review of 1.3 million pregnancies from the Medicaid Analytic eXtract found no increased risk for any malformation with ACE-inhibitor exposure after adjusting for confounding factors such as diabetes .

250
00:33:10,218 --> 00:33:16,593
Thus, women with inadvertent first-trimester exposure to these medications may be reassured.

251
00:33:16,756 --> 00:33:31,781
Importantly, given the many therapeutic options for treating hypertension during pregnancy, discussed in Chapter 53 (p. 949), ACE inhibitors and angiotensin receptor-blocking drugs should be avoided in pregnancy.

252
00:33:31,731 --> 00:33:34,868
# Antifungal Medications.

253
00:33:34,868 --> 00:33:44,643
From this class of drugs, fluconazole has been associated with a pattern of congenital malformations resembling the autosomal recessive Antley-Bixler syndrome.

254
00:33:44,643 --> 00:33:52,906
Abnormalities include oral clefts, abnormal facies, and cardiac, skull, long-bone, and joint abnormalities.

255
00:33:52,906 --> 00:34:01,831
Such findings have been reported only with chronic, first-trimester, high-dose treatment at doses of 400 to 800 mg daily.

256
00:34:01,831 --> 00:34:15,856
Regarding low-dose treatment of vulvovaginal candidiasis, the Motherisk Program conducted a systematic review of pregnancies with first-trimester oral fluconazole exposure of 150 or 300 mg in total .

257
00:34:15,856 --> 00:34:24,331
The overall risk for birth defects was not greater, although a small increase in rates of cardiac malformations could not be excluded.

258
00:34:24,331 --> 00:34:36,606
A population-based cohort study from Denmark identified a threefold greater risk for tetralogy of Fallot following exposure to low-dose fluconazole (Mølgaard-Nielsen, 2013).

259
00:34:36,606 --> 00:34:42,431
The birth prevalence of tetralogy of Fallot rose from 3 to 10 cases per 10,000.

260
00:34:42,431 --> 00:34:56,106
Notably, investigators did not identify increased risks for 14 other birth defects previously associated with exposure to high-dose azole antifungal agents (Mølgaard-Nielsen, 2013).

261
00:34:56,106 --> 00:35:06,506
A subsequent review of more than 37,000 pregnancies with first-trimester fluconazole exposure did not identify an association with cardiac abnormalities .

262
00:35:06,506 --> 00:35:15,356
Based on reported risks, we do not perform detailed sonography or fetal echocardiography following low-dose fluconazole exposure.

263
00:35:15,356 --> 00:35:18,568
# Antiinflammatory Agents.

264
00:35:18,568 --> 00:35:27,693
Nonsteroidal Antiinflammatory Drugs This drug class includes both aspirin and traditional NSAIDs such as ibuprofen and indomethacin.

265
00:35:27,693 --> 00:35:32,243
They exert their effects by inhibiting prostaglandin synthesis.

266
00:35:32,243 --> 00:35:46,156
In a report from the NBDPS, at least 20 percent of pregnant women recall first-trimester NSAID use, particularly ibuprofen and aspirin, and such exposure is not a major risk factor for birth defects .

267
00:35:46,156 --> 00:35:55,893
When taken in late pregnancy, however, indomethacin may cause constriction of the fetal ductus arteriosus and subsequent pulmonary hypertension.

268
00:35:55,893 --> 00:36:02,206
Fetal ductal constriction is more likely with third trimester use that exceeds 72 hours.

269
00:36:02,206 --> 00:36:13,818
The drug also may decrease fetal urine production and amnionic fluid volume (Rasanen, 1995; van der Heijden, 1994; Walker, 1994).

270
00:36:13,818 --> 00:36:28,656
In one systematic review, indomethacin tocolysis was associated with a 1.5-fold risk for bronchopulmonary dysplasia, intraventricular hemorrhage, and necrotizing enterocolitis (Hammers, 2015a,b).

271
00:36:28,656 --> 00:36:44,856
Based on a review of 35 cases of oligohydramnios or neonatal kidney problems reported to the FDA Adverse Event Reporting System database, the FDA recommends that pregnant women ≥20 weeks’ gestation avoid NSAID use .

272
00:36:44,856 --> 00:36:52,306
If NSAIDs are needed for more than 48 hours, ultrasound evaluation of amnionic fluid should be considered.

273
00:36:52,306 --> 00:37:01,418
Oligohydramnios usually resolves within 6 days of discontinuing NSAIDs, but there are cases in which infants have died of renal failure.

274
00:37:01,418 --> 00:37:12,168
With aspirin, a low dosage of 100 mg daily or less does not confer a greater risk for constriction of the ductus arteriosus or for adverse infant outcomes .

275
00:37:12,168 --> 00:37:19,618
As with other NSAIDs, however, high-dose aspirin use should be avoided, particularly in the third trimester.

276
00:37:19,618 --> 00:37:25,443
Leflunomide This is a pyrimidine-synthesis inhibitor used to treat rheumatoid arthritis.

277
00:37:25,443 --> 00:37:29,568
Leflunomide is considered contraindicated in pregnancy.

278
00:37:29,568 --> 00:37:40,618
In rats and rabbits, leflunomide results in hydrocephalus, eye anomalies, skeletal anomalies, and embryo death when given at or below human-equivalent doses .

279
00:37:40,618 --> 00:37:49,043
The active metabolite, teriflunomide, is detectable in plasma for up to 2 years following discontinuation of the medication.

280
00:37:49,043 --> 00:38:02,956
Women who become pregnant while taking leflunomide, and even those of childbearing potential who have discontinued it, are recommended to undergo an accelerated drug elimination procedure with either cholestyramine or activated charcoal .

281
00:38:02,956 --> 00:38:05,693
Reassuringly, studies of

282
00:38:05,860 --> 00:38:14,597
exposed human pregnancies have not confirmed these teratogenic effects (Berard, 2018; Chambers, 2010).

283
00:38:14,547 --> 00:38:24,210
In a cohort of 60 women with first-trimester leflunomide exposure who completed cholestyramine washout, the rate of birth defects was not increased .

284
00:38:24,210 --> 00:38:33,685
A recent review of more than 500 exposed pregnancies found that the rate of major birth defects was 3 percent, similar to the general population .

285
00:38:33,685 --> 00:38:37,772
The rate of spontaneous abortion also was not increased.

286
00:38:37,772 --> 00:38:41,585
# Antimicrobial and Antiviral Drugs.

287
00:38:41,585 --> 00:38:47,872
Medications used to treat infections are among those most frequently administered during pregnancy.

288
00:38:47,872 --> 00:38:52,672
Over the years, experience has accrued regarding their general safety.

289
00:38:52,672 --> 00:38:59,647
With few exceptions, commonly used antimicrobial agents are without embryo-fetal safety concerns.

290
00:38:59,647 --> 00:39:08,447
Nitrofurantoin From NBDPS results, first-trimester nitrofurantoin exposure is linked to a twofold risk for cleft lip .

291
00:39:08,447 --> 00:39:20,535
Considering that the birth prevalence of clefts approximates 1 case per 1000, the likelihood that a nitrofurantoinexposed fetus would not have a cleft would thus be 998 per 1000.

292
00:39:20,535 --> 00:39:28,672
For other birth defects, initial associations with this antibiotic did not persist in the final NBDPS cohort .

293
00:39:28,672 --> 00:39:36,647
In one systematic review of nitrofurantoin exposure in pregnancy, results of cohort and case-control studies differed .

294
00:39:36,647 --> 00:39:49,585
Five cohort studies included 9275 exposed pregnancies and nearly 1.5 million unexposed pregnancies, and the review found no higher risk for any malformation.

295
00:39:49,585 --> 00:40:01,047
However, among three case-control studies that had nearly 40,000 cases matched with 130,000 controls, the rate of hypoplastic left heart syndrome was threefold greater .

296
00:40:01,047 --> 00:40:09,010
For context, this increase in risk would result in a birth prevalence of fewer than 1 case per 1000 exposed infants.

297
00:40:09,010 --> 00:40:24,022
Nitrofurantoin is considered contraindicated in pregnant women with known or suspected glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency because of risk for hemolytic anemia and other hematologic abnormalities.

298
00:40:24,022 --> 00:40:36,810
In the absence of G6PD deficiency, the There are no concerns with second- or third-trimester nitrofurantoin use, and first-trimester use is appropriate if no suitable alternatives are available.

299
00:40:36,810 --> 00:40:43,622
Sulfonamides These drugs are often combined with trimethoprim and used to treat infections during pregnancy.

300
00:40:43,622 --> 00:40:50,547
One indication is treatment of methicillinresistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) infection.

301
00:40:50,547 --> 00:41:06,310
The NBDPS, which included 107 pregnancies with periconceptional trimethoprimsulfamethoxazole exposure and birth defects, identified a fivefold greater risk to have offspring with esophageal atresia or diaphragmatic hernia .

302
00:41:06,310 --> 00:41:17,972
Similar to findings with nitrofurantoin exposure, this degree of increase would confer a risk of approximately 1 case per 1000 exposed infants for these selected birth defects.

303
00:41:17,972 --> 00:41:22,785
However, these findings have not been corroborated by other reports.

304
00:41:22,785 --> 00:41:34,772
One review from the Medication Exposure in Pregnancy Risk Evaluation Program included more than 7500 infants with first-trimester exposure to trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole .

305
00:41:34,772 --> 00:41:44,797
Compared with either unexposed infants or those exposed to penicillins or cephalosporins, no higher risk for any congenital abnormality was identified.

306
00:41:44,797 --> 00:41:52,960
Given this reassuring data, we consider sulfonamides appropriate for first-trimester use if suitable alternatives are lacking.

307
00:41:52,960 --> 00:42:04,635
Sulfonamides are considered contraindicated in pregnant women with known or suspected G6PD deficiency because of risk for hemolytic anemia and other hematologic abnormalities.

308
00:42:04,635 --> 00:42:10,085
Additionally, sulfonamides displace bilirubin from protein-binding sites.

309
00:42:10,085 --> 00:42:18,272
Thus, if given near the time of preterm delivery, these agents theoretically might worsen neonatal hyperbilirubinemia.

310
00:42:18,272 --> 00:42:30,210
However, a population-based review of more than 800,000 births from Denmark found no association between exposure to sulfamethoxazole in late pregnancy and neonatal jaundice .

311
00:42:30,210 --> 00:42:34,735
Tetracyclines These drugs are not commonly used in pregnant women.

312
00:42:34,735 --> 00:42:42,272
They have been associated with yellowish-brown discoloration of the deciduous teeth when used after 25 weeks’ gestation.

313
00:42:42,272 --> 00:42:50,147
The risk for subsequent dental caries is not increased (Billings, 2004; Kutscher, 1966).

314
00:42:50,308 --> 00:42:58,458
Doxycycline has a reduced ability to chelate calcium orthophosphate compared with other tetracyclines and is preferred if needed.

315
00:42:58,408 --> 00:43:07,220
A systematic review of doxycycline-exposed pregnancies identified no higher rates of either birth defects or staining of deciduous teeth .

316
00:43:07,220 --> 00:43:17,470
Ribavirin This antiviral nucleoside analogue is a component of therapy for hepatitis C infection, discussed in Chapter 58 (p. 1038).

317
00:43:17,470 --> 00:43:25,545
Ribavirin causes birth defects in multiple animal species at doses significantly lower than those recommended for human use.

318
00:43:25,545 --> 00:43:32,995
Reported malformations include skull, palate, eye, skeleton, and gastrointestinal abnormalities.

319
00:43:32,995 --> 00:43:40,408
The drug has a half-life of 12 days and persists in extravascular compartments following therapy discontinuation.

320
00:43:40,408 --> 00:43:49,470
Treated women must use two forms of contraception and have monthly pregnancy tests while on therapy and for 6 months following drug discontinuation .

321
00:43:49,470 --> 00:43:54,470
Ribavirin use is also contraindicated in men whose partners are pregnant.

322
00:43:54,470 --> 00:43:57,520
# Antineoplastic Agents.

323
00:43:57,520 --> 00:44:07,333
Cancer management in pregnancy includes many chemotherapeutic agents generally considered to be at least potentially toxic to the embryo, fetus, or both.

324
00:44:07,333 --> 00:44:15,845
For the many novel polyclonal antibody therapies designated as antineoplastics, there is little information concerning safety.

325
00:44:15,845 --> 00:44:27,808
The National Toxicology Program (2013) conducted a review of 300 pregnancies with first-trimester exposure and identified major malformations in 14 percent.

326
00:44:27,808 --> 00:44:34,270
Treatment of cancer in pregnancy is discussed in Chapter 66 (p. 1163).

327
00:44:34,270 --> 00:44:40,983
Risks associated with selected agents for which experience in pregnancy has accrued are considered next.

328
00:44:40,983 --> 00:44:51,895
Cyclophosphamide This alkylating agent inflicts a chemical insult on developing fetal tissues and leads to cell death and heritable DNA alterations in surviving cells.

329
00:44:51,895 --> 00:45:05,608
Pregnancy loss rates are greater, and reported fetal abnormalities include skeletal anomalies, limb defects, cleft palate, and eye abnormalities (Enns, 1999; Kirshon, 1988).

330
00:45:05,608 --> 00:45:10,670
The risk for a major abnormality is estimated to be 18 percent .

331
00:45:10,670 --> 00:45:15,820
Surviving infants may have growth abnormalities and developmental delays.

332
00:45:15,820 --> 00:45:22,658
Environmental exposure among health-care workers is associated with a higher risk for spontaneous abortion.

333
00:45:22,658 --> 00:45:27,445
Methotrexate This folic-acid antagonist is a potent teratogen.

334
00:45:27,445 --> 00:45:37,658
It is used for cancer chemotherapy, immunosuppression of autoimmune diseases and psoriasis, nonsurgical treatment of ectopic pregnancy, and medical abortion.

335
00:45:37,658 --> 00:45:47,520
It acts similarly to aminopterin, which is no longer used clinically, and can cause defects known collectively as the fetal methotrexate-aminopterin syndrome.

336
00:45:47,520 --> 00:45:57,720
Associated craniofacial abnormalities include craniosynostosis with a “clover-leaf” skull, wide nasal bridge, low-set ears, and micrognathia .

337
00:45:57,720 --> 00:46:05,245
Exposure has also been linked to central nervous system abnormalities, cardiac defects, and limb anomalies .

338
00:46:05,245 --> 00:46:08,433
Intellectual impairment has been described.

339
00:46:08,433 --> 00:46:16,295
The embryo is thought to be most vulnerable at 8 to 10 weeks postconception and at dosages of at least 10 mg/week.

340
00:46:16,295 --> 00:46:19,783
However, this is not universally accepted .

341
00:46:19,783 --> 00:46:25,658
Because of its distribution, methotrexate can remain in the body for prolonged periods.

342
00:46:25,658 --> 00:46:29,845
Thus, preconceptional exposure is not without risk.

343
00:46:29,845 --> 00:46:39,308
The standard 50-mg/m2 dose given to treat ectopic pregnancy or to induce abortion exceeds the 10-mg/week threshold dose.

344
00:46:39,308 --> 00:46:54,533
Reports describe cardiac anomalies, particularly conotruncal defects, in intrauterine pregnancies inadvertently treated with methotrexate for suspected ectopic pregnancy (Dawson, 2014; Hyoun, 2012).

345
00:46:54,533 --> 00:47:03,095
Tamoxifen This nonsteroidal selective estrogen-receptor modulator (SERM) is used as an adjuvant to treat breast cancer.

346
00:47:03,095 --> 00:47:15,208
In animal studies, tamoxifen has been associated with malformations similar to those caused by diethylstilbestrol (DES) exposure in rodents, including vaginal adenosis.

347
00:47:15,208 --> 00:47:24,020
One review of 167 pregnancies reported a fetal abnormality in 13 percent but emphasized that evidence was limited .

348
00:47:24,020 --> 00:47:29,658
Abnormalities have included ambiguous genitalia and craniofacial anomalies.

349
00:47:29,658 --> 00:47:37,858
Trastuzumab This is a recombinant monoclonal antibody directed to the human epidermal growth factor receptor 2 (HER2)

350
00:47:38,020 --> 00:47:39,595
protein.

351
00:47:39,545 --> 00:47:47,695
Used to treat breast and gastric cancers that express HER2 protein, this drug has not been associated with fetal malformations.

352
00:47:47,695 --> 00:47:59,982
However, postmarketing surveillance identified cases of oligohydramnios sequence resulting in pulmonary hypoplasia, renal failure, skeletal abnormalities, and neonatal death .

353
00:47:59,982 --> 00:48:08,757
Surveillance for these complications is recommended for exposed pregnancies and for those treated at any time in the 7 months prior to conception.

354
00:48:08,757 --> 00:48:17,457
A trastuzumab pregnancy exposure registry and a pregnancy pharmacovigilance program have been established to monitor pregnancy outcomes.

355
00:48:17,457 --> 00:48:22,557
These warnings also apply to those treated with ado-trastuzumab emtansine.

356
00:48:22,557 --> 00:48:25,995
# Endothelin-receptor Antagonists.

357
00:48:25,995 --> 00:48:34,732
Bosentan, ambrisentan, and macitentan are three endothelin-receptor antagonists used to treat pulmonary arterial hypertension .

358
00:48:34,732 --> 00:48:40,120
The endothelin-receptor signaling pathway is important for neural-crest development.

359
00:48:40,120 --> 00:48:51,545
Mice deficient in endothelin receptors develop neural-crest cell defects that include craniofacial and cardiac outflow tract abnormalities (de Raaf, 2015).

360
00:48:51,545 --> 00:49:00,307
Each of these three agents has been found to cause similar birth defects in multiple animal species (Janssen, 2019a,b).

361
00:49:00,307 --> 00:49:02,970
No human data are available.

362
00:49:02,970 --> 00:49:18,782
Endothelin-receptor antagonists may be obtained only through restricted access programs, each of which has stringent requirements that include contraception and monthly pregnancy testing (Gilead, 2019; Janssen, 2019a,b).

363
00:49:18,782 --> 00:49:22,045
# Immunosuppressant Medications.

364
00:49:22,045 --> 00:49:28,757
Selected autoimmune diseases and their treatment are reviewed in Chapter 62 (p. 1109).

365
00:49:28,757 --> 00:49:36,195
Corticosteroids Glucocorticoids and mineralocorticoids have antiinflammatory and immunosuppressive actions.

366
00:49:36,195 --> 00:49:41,470
They are used to treat serious disorders such as asthma and autoimmune disease.

367
00:49:41,470 --> 00:49:48,695
Corticosteroids have been associated with orofacial clefts in animal studies, but the absolute risk is small.

368
00:49:48,695 --> 00:50:02,782
In a metaanalysis of case-control studies by the Motherisk Program, systemic corticosteroid exposure was associated with a threefold rate increase, conferring an absolute risk of 3 clefts per 1000 exposed fetuses .

369
00:50:02,782 --> 00:50:11,732
A 10-year prospective cohort study by the same group, however, did not identify an increased overall risk for major malformations.

370
00:50:11,732 --> 00:50:18,345
Based on these findings, corticosteroids are not considered to represent a major teratogenic risk.

371
00:50:18,345 --> 00:50:26,382
Potential risks associated with prednisone or methylprednisolone are considered to be lower than those with other corticosteroids.

372
00:50:26,382 --> 00:50:37,320
Prednisolone, the active metabolite of prednisone, is itself metabolized and rendered inactive by the placental enzyme 11β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase 2 .

373
00:50:37,320 --> 00:50:41,270
Thus, it is thought to reach the fetus less effectively.

374
00:50:41,270 --> 00:50:50,932
Mycophenolate Mofetil This inosine monophosphate dehydrogenase inhibitor, and a related agent, mycophenolic acid, are immunosuppressants.

375
00:50:50,932 --> 00:50:57,132
They are used to prevent rejection in organ-transplant recipients and to treat autoimmune disease .

376
00:50:57,132 --> 00:51:00,320
Mycophenolate is a potent teratogen.

377
00:51:00,320 --> 00:51:14,895
From the National Transplantation Pregnancy Registry, of pregnancies in which mycophenolate was not discontinued until after the first trimester, birth defects complicated 30 percent, and another 30 percent spontaneously aborted .

378
00:51:14,895 --> 00:51:26,007
One prospective review by the European Network of Teratology Information Services similarly identified a spontaneous loss rate of nearly 30 percent in exposed pregnancies.

379
00:51:26,007 --> 00:51:30,470
More than 20 percent of liveborn infants had major anomalies .

380
00:51:30,470 --> 00:51:36,195
Many affected infants have a pattern of defects termed mycophenolate embryopathy.

381
00:51:36,195 --> 00:51:51,145
This includes microtia, auditory canal atresia, clefts, coloboma and other eye anomalies, short fingers with hypoplastic nails, and cardiac defects (Anderka, 2009; Merlob, 2009).

382
00:51:51,145 --> 00:52:00,807
A Risk Evaluation and Mitigation Strategy (REMS) has been developed for mycophenolate prescribers who treat women with reproductive potential.

383
00:52:00,807 --> 00:52:11,945
REMS are safety strategies mandated by the FDA to help manage known risks associated with a medicine yet still allow patients to have access to the benefits of a given drug.

384
00:52:11,945 --> 00:52:13,870
# Lead.

385
00:52:13,870 --> 00:52:17,532
Lead crosses the placenta via passive diffusion.

386
00:52:17,532 --> 00:52:21,470
Prenatal lead exposure is associated with fetal-growth

387
00:52:21,628 --> 00:52:25,540
impairment and with childhood neurodevelopmental delays.

388
00:52:25,490 --> 00:52:32,340
According to the CDC (2010), no level of lead exposure is considered safe in pregnancy.

389
00:52:32,340 --> 00:52:38,528
Although routine testing is not recommended, risk factors should be assessed in all pregnancies .

390
00:52:38,528 --> 00:52:44,503
Care and testing for atrisk pregnancies is discussed in Chapter 10 (p. 188).

391
00:52:44,503 --> 00:52:46,653
# Mercury.

392
00:52:46,653 --> 00:52:51,265
The developing nervous system is particularly susceptible to mercury.

393
00:52:51,265 --> 00:53:04,603
In the 1950s and early 1960s, children born near Minamata Bay, Japan were found to have congenital Minamata disease—severe neurological abnormalities from methylmercury exposure in utero .

394
00:53:04,603 --> 00:53:10,028
Prenatal exposure causes disturbances in neuronal cell division and migration.

395
00:53:10,028 --> 00:53:21,053
This leads to a range of defects from microcephaly to intellectual disability, choreo-athetoid movement abnormalities, motor delays, and behavioral abnormalities .

396
00:53:21,053 --> 00:53:28,453
The principal concern for prenatal mercury exposure is the consumption of certain species of large fish and seafood .

397
00:53:28,453 --> 00:53:31,965
Methylmercury cannot be eliminated by cooking.

398
00:53:31,965 --> 00:53:35,915
It crosses the placenta and can accumulate in the fetus.

399
00:53:35,915 --> 00:53:48,515
The FDA (2019) advises that pregnant women and breastfeeding mothers avoid consumption of king mackerel, marlin, orange roughy, shark, swordfish, tilefish, and bigeye tuna.

400
00:53:48,515 --> 00:53:51,715
# Psychiatric Medications.

401
00:53:51,715 --> 00:54:03,890
Treatment of psychiatric illness in pregnancy, including a discussion of the risks and benefits of various psychiatric medications, is described in Chapter 64 (p. 1142).

402
00:54:03,890 --> 00:54:10,028
Antipsychotic Medications No antipsychotic medications are considered teratogenic.

403
00:54:10,028 --> 00:54:26,765
Exposed neonates can manifest abnormal extrapyramidal muscle movements and withdrawal symptoms that include agitation, abnormally enhanced or diminished muscle tone, tremor, agitation, somnolence, respiratory abnormalities, and feeding difficulty.

404
00:54:26,765 --> 00:54:30,190
Such findings are nonspecific and transient.

405
00:54:30,190 --> 00:54:36,053
An FDA (2017) alert cited all medications in this class.

406
00:54:36,053 --> 00:54:46,453
Included are older medications like haloperidol and chlorpromazine and newer medications such as aripiprazole, olanzapine, quetiapine, and risperidone.

407
00:54:46,453 --> 00:54:56,528
Lithium This medication has been associated with Ebstein anomaly, a rare cardiac abnormality that otherwise complicates only 1 per 20,000 births.

408
00:54:56,528 --> 00:55:07,478
Ebstein anomaly is characterized by apical displacement of the tricuspid valve, often resulting in severe tricuspid regurgitation and marked right atrial enlargement.

409
00:55:07,478 --> 00:55:14,965
A report from the Lithium Baby Registry initially suggested that the risk for Ebstein anomaly was as high as 3 percent.

410
00:55:14,965 --> 00:55:31,653
However, subsequent series have identified an attributable risk for Ebstein anomaly and cooccurring right-sided cardiac anomalies of only 1 to 4 cases per 1000 exposed pregnancies (Patorno, 2017; Yacobi, 2008).

411
00:55:31,653 --> 00:55:39,515
The NBDPS found that just 1 of 135 cases of Ebstein anomaly occurred in the setting of lithium exposure .

412
00:55:39,515 --> 00:55:44,128
Neonatal lithium toxicity stems from exposure near delivery.

413
00:55:44,128 --> 00:55:53,715
The manufacturer recommends that if possible, the dosage should be decreased or drug discontinued 2 to 3 days prior to delivery to reduce this risk .

414
00:55:53,715 --> 00:56:04,065
Findings may persist for up to 2 weeks and may include cardiac arrhythmias, hypoglycemia, nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, and a “floppy infant syndrome” .

415
00:56:04,065 --> 00:56:12,540
The latter may include hypotonia, respiratory distress or apnea, bradycardia, cyanosis, and feeding difficulties .

416
00:56:12,540 --> 00:56:23,165
Selective Serotonin- and Norepinephrine-reuptake Inhibitors These medications have been studied more than almost any other, apart from Bendectin (p. 146).

417
00:56:23,165 --> 00:56:27,278
As a class, they are not considered major teratogens .

418
00:56:27,278 --> 00:56:37,890
The one exception is paroxetine, which has been associated with a slightly higher risk for cardiac anomalies, particularly atrial and ventricular septal defects.

419
00:56:37,890 --> 00:56:49,453
Metaanalyses have identified a pooled odds ratio of just 1.3 for paroxetine and cardiac abnormalities (Berard, 2016; Wurst, 2010).

420
00:56:49,453 --> 00:57:03,315
However, a review of nearly 1 million pregnancies from the nationwide Medicaid Analytic eXtract identified no significant association between paroxetine use in the first trimester and fetal cardiac abnormalities .

421
00:57:03,315 --> 00:57:12,565
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019f) recommends that women planning pregnancy avoid paroxetine.

422
00:57:12,565 --> 00:57:14,003
Fetal

423
00:57:14,164 --> 00:57:19,939
echocardiography should be considered for those with first-trimester paroxetine exposure.

424
00:57:19,889 --> 00:57:32,151
Neonatal effects have been associated with prenatal exposure to selective serotonin-reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) and selective norepinephrine-reuptake inhibitors (SNRIs).

425
00:57:32,151 --> 00:57:47,451
Approximately 25 percent of neonates exposed to SSRIs in late pregnancy manifest one or more nonspecific findings considered to represent poor neonatal adaptation (Costei, 2002; Jordan, 2008).

426
00:57:47,451 --> 00:58:03,601
Collectively termed the neonatal behavioral syndrome, findings can include jitteriness, irritability, hyper- or hypotonia, feeding abnormalities, vomiting, hypoglycemia, thermoregulatory instability, and respiratory abnormalities.

427
00:58:03,601 --> 00:58:10,926
Fortunately, these neonatal behaviors are typically mild and self-limited and last approximately 2 days.

428
00:58:10,926 --> 00:58:23,789
Jordan and coworkers (2008) reported that affected newborns were not more likely to require a higher level of care, to experience respiratory abnormalities, or to have prolonged hospitalization.

429
00:58:23,789 --> 00:58:31,789
Rarely, neonates exposed to SSRIs in late pregnancy have demonstrated more severe adaptation abnormalities .

430
00:58:31,789 --> 00:58:42,614
Another concern with late-pregnancy exposure is the possible association of SSRI medications with persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn (PPHN).

431
00:58:42,614 --> 00:58:47,989
The baseline incidence approximates 2 cases per 1000 term newborns.

432
00:58:47,989 --> 00:58:56,314
PPHN is characterized by elevated pulmonary vascular resistance with right-to-left shunting and resultant hypoxemia.

433
00:58:56,314 --> 00:59:11,651
Two population-based cohort studies—together involving more than 5 million pregnancies—identified an attributable risk of only 1 to 2 cases per 1000 births (Huybrechts, 2015; Kieler, 2012).

434
00:59:11,651 --> 00:59:18,351
Moreover, cases of PPHN associated with SSRI medication have not been severe .

435
00:59:18,351 --> 00:59:20,626
# Retinoids.

436
00:59:20,626 --> 00:59:25,289
Vitamin A derivatives are among the most potent human teratogens.

437
00:59:25,289 --> 00:59:39,176
By inhibiting neural-crest cell migration during embryogenesis, they create a pattern of cranial neural-crest defects—termed retinoic acid embryopathy— that involve the CNS, face, heart, and thymus .

438
00:59:39,176 --> 00:59:53,751
Specific anomalies may include ventriculomegaly, maldevelopment of the facial bones or cranium, microtia or anotia, micrognathia, cleft palate, conotruncal heart defects, and thymic aplasia or hypoplasia.

439
00:59:53,751 --> 00:59:55,976
Figure 8-3.

440
00:59:55,976 --> 00:59:58,814
Retinoic acid embryopathy.

441
00:59:58,814 --> 01:00:04,276
A. Bilateral microtia or anotia with stenosis of external ear canal.

442
01:00:04,276 --> 01:00:08,951
B. Flat, depressed nasal bridge and ocular hypertelorism.

443
01:00:08,951 --> 01:00:13,364
Reproduced with permission from permission from Dr. Edward Lammer.

444
01:00:13,364 --> 01:00:17,814
Acitretin This retinoid is used to treat severe psoriasis.

445
01:00:17,814 --> 01:00:29,776
It was introduced to replace etretinate, a lipophilic retinoid with such a long half-life (120 days) that birth defects resulted more than 2 years after therapy was discontinued.

446
01:00:29,776 --> 01:00:38,026
Although acitretin has a short half-life, it is metabolized to etretinate, and thus remains in the body for prolonged periods .

447
01:00:38,026 --> 01:00:45,026
To obviate exposure, the manufacturer of acitretin has developed a pregnancy risk management program.

448
01:00:45,026 --> 01:00:56,989
Called “Do Your P.A.R.T”—pregnancy prevention actively required during and after treatment, this program promotes a delay of conception for at least 3 years following therapy discontinuation.

449
01:00:56,989 --> 01:01:02,601
Bexarotene This retinoid is used to treat refractory cutaneous T-cell lymphoma.

450
01:01:02,601 --> 01:01:13,089
When given to rats in doses comparable to those for human therapy, fetuses developed eye and ear abnormalities, cleft palate, and incomplete ossification.

451
01:01:13,089 --> 01:01:24,739
For a woman to receive bexarotene, the manufacturer requires two forms of contraception that are initiated 1 month before therapy and continued for 1 month after it is discontinued.

452
01:01:24,739 --> 01:01:28,889
This is coupled with monthly pregnancy testing during treatment .

453
01:01:28,889 --> 01:01:39,151
Males who have partners who could become pregnant are advised to use condoms during sexual intercourse while taking bexarotene and for 1 month after discontinuing therapy.

454
01:01:39,151 --> 01:01:46,939
Isotretinoin 13-cis-Retinoic acid is a vitamin A isomer that stimulates epithelial cell differentiation.

455
01:01:46,939 --> 01:01:51,226
It is primarily used to treat recalcitrant nodular acne.

456
01:01:51,226 --> 01:01:59,326
Firsttrimester exposure is associated with a high rate of pregnancy loss, and up to a third of infants have malformations .

457
01:01:59,326 --> 01:02:03,839
The iPLEDGE program is an FDA-mandated REMS for

458
01:02:03,988 --> 01:02:09,725
isotretinoin and is found at www.ipledgeprogram.com.

459
01:02:09,675 --> 01:02:20,213
This web-based, restricted-distribution program requires participation for all patients, physicians, and pharmacies to help eliminate embryo–fetal exposure.

460
01:02:20,213 --> 01:02:31,275
Other countries have instituted similar programs, however, inadvertent exposure remains a global concern (AutretLeca, 2010; Crijns, 2011).

461
01:02:31,275 --> 01:02:40,813
Topical Retinoids These compounds, initially used to treat acne, have become so popular for sun-damage treatment that they are called cosmeceuticals .

462
01:02:40,813 --> 01:02:46,838
The most commonly used topical agents are tretinoin, isotretinoin, and adapalene.

463
01:02:46,838 --> 01:02:52,388
Systemic absorption is low, and this argues against plausible teratogenicity.

464
01:02:52,388 --> 01:03:04,338
Isolated case reports have described malformations following topical tretinoin, and it is unknown whether this is due to variability in absorption or perhaps potential individual susceptibility .

465
01:03:04,338 --> 01:03:15,875
A prospective study by the European Network of Teratology Information Services found no higher rates of birth defects or spontaneous losses, and no case of retinoid embryopathy .

466
01:03:15,875 --> 01:03:30,000
A systematic review of 635 pregnancies exposed to topical retinoids identified no higher risk for congenital malformations, spontaneous abortion, stillbirth, low birthweight, or preterm delivery .

467
01:03:30,000 --> 01:03:39,000
Notably, the manufacturer of tazarotene cautions that application over a sufficient body surface area could be comparable to oral treatment.

468
01:03:39,000 --> 01:03:42,975
Accordingly, its use in pregnancy is not recommended .

469
01:03:42,975 --> 01:03:46,975
Vitamin A There are two natural forms of vitamin A.

470
01:03:46,975 --> 01:03:56,950
First, provitamin A carotenoids, which include beta-carotene, alpha-carotene, and beta-cryptoxanthin, are precursors found in fruits and vegetables.

471
01:03:56,950 --> 01:04:00,525
The body converts carotenoids into vitamin A.

472
01:04:00,525 --> 01:04:03,938
They have never been shown to cause birth defects .

473
01:04:03,938 --> 01:04:15,488
Second, preformed vitamin A, also called retinol, has been associated with cranial neural-crest defects when more than 10,000 IU per day is consumed in the first trimester .

474
01:04:15,488 --> 01:04:23,863
Preformed vitamin A is present in liver, fish oils, milk, eggs, and in vitamin pills and dietary supplements.

475
01:04:23,863 --> 01:04:31,763
It seems reasonable to avoid doses of preformed preparations that exceed the recommended 3000 IU daily allowance .

476
01:04:31,763 --> 01:04:34,275
# Sex Hormones.

477
01:04:34,275 --> 01:04:42,713
Selected functions and effects of male and female hormones on the developing fetus are reviewed in Chapter 3 (p. 31).

478
01:04:42,713 --> 01:04:53,400
Androgen exposure may cause varying degrees of virilization in female fetuses and may result in ambiguous genitalia (Fig. 3-3, p. 34).

479
01:04:53,400 --> 01:04:56,400
# Thalidomide and Analogues.

480
01:04:56,400 --> 01:05:07,263
Possibly the most notorious human teratogen, thalidomide causes malformations in 20 percent of fetuses exposed between 34 and 50 days menstrual age.

481
01:05:07,263 --> 01:05:14,488
The characteristic malformation is phocomelia—an absence or underdevelopment of one or more long bones.

482
01:05:14,488 --> 01:05:21,025
As a result, hands or feet may attach to the trunk, occasionally by a small rudimentary bone.

483
01:05:21,025 --> 01:05:32,363
Cardiac defects, gastrointestinal abnormalities, external ear and eye malformations, and other limb-reduction defects also are common following thalidomide exposure.

484
01:05:32,363 --> 01:05:39,288
The manufacturer reports that up to 40 percent of affected newborns do not survive the neonatal period .

485
01:05:39,288 --> 01:05:48,088
Thalidomide was marketed outside the United States from 1956 to 1960, before its teratogenicity was appreciated.

486
01:05:48,088 --> 01:05:55,813
The ensuing disaster, with thousands of affected children, was instructive of several important teratological principles.

487
01:05:55,813 --> 01:06:02,813
First, the placenta is not an effective barrier to the transfer of toxic substances from mother to embryo .

488
01:06:02,813 --> 01:06:09,850
Second, different species manifest considerable variability in their susceptibility to drugs and chemicals.

489
01:06:09,850 --> 01:06:16,850
Namely, thalidomide produced no defects in multiple rodent species and was assumed to be safe for humans.

490
01:06:16,850 --> 01:06:21,988
Last, timing of exposure may be closely related to the type of defect .

491
01:06:21,988 --> 01:06:38,875
For example, upper-limb amelia may develop with thalidomide exposure during days 24 to 30 postconception, upper-limb phocomelia with exposure during days 24 to 33, and lower-limb phocomelia with exposure during days 27 to 33.

492
01:06:38,875 --> 01:06:48,025
Thalidomide was first approved in the United States in 1999 and currently is used to treat erythema leprosum nodosum and multiple myeloma .

493
01:06:48,025 --> 01:06:51,100
The FDA has mandated a web-based,

494
01:06:51,268 --> 01:07:02,205
restricteddistribution program for thalidomide, called THALOMID REMS, which is required before patients, physicians, and pharmacies can access the medication.

495
01:07:02,155 --> 01:07:06,555
Lenalidomide and pomalidomide are analogues of thalidomide.

496
01:07:06,555 --> 01:07:10,105
Both cross the placenta in animal species.

497
01:07:10,105 --> 01:07:16,205
Lenalidomide is used to treat some types of myelodysplastic syndrome and multiple myeloma.

498
01:07:16,205 --> 01:07:21,043
It has been found to cause thalidomide-like limb abnormalities in monkeys .

499
01:07:21,043 --> 01:07:29,668
Pomalidomide is used to treat refractory multiple myeloma and acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS)-related Kaposi sarcoma.

500
01:07:29,668 --> 01:07:32,955
It is teratogenic in rats and rabbits .

501
01:07:32,955 --> 01:07:39,493
Restricted-distribution programs similar to those used for thalidomide have been developed for these analogues.

502
01:07:39,493 --> 01:07:42,393
# Thyroid Medications.

503
01:07:42,393 --> 01:07:47,180
Methimazole This thionamide drug is used to treat hyperthyroidism.

504
01:07:47,180 --> 01:07:56,693
Methimazole has been associated with a 2-fold increased risk for aplasia cutis congenita, as well as choanal atresia and esophageal atresia .

505
01:07:56,693 --> 01:08:01,568
This constellation of findings has been termed methimazole embryopathy.

506
01:08:01,568 --> 01:08:11,305
Aplasia cutis is a rare abnormality characterized by a full-thickness skin defect, usually the scalp, occasionally with a defect of underlying bone.

507
01:08:11,305 --> 01:08:21,780
In a study of more than 1000 methimazole-treated pregnancies, Yoshihara and colleagues (2015) identified the embryopathy in 1 to 2 percent.

508
01:08:21,780 --> 01:08:26,968
Treatment of hyperthyroidism in pregnancy is reviewed in Chapter 61.

509
01:08:26,968 --> 01:08:36,968
Radioiodine Radioactive iodine-131 is used for treatment of thyroid cancer and thyrotoxicosis and for diagnostic thyroid scanning.

510
01:08:36,968 --> 01:08:45,355
It is also a component of iodine-131 tositumomab therapy, which is employed to treat a type of nonHodgkin lymphoma.

511
01:08:45,355 --> 01:08:55,468
Radioiodine is contraindicated during pregnancy because it readily crosses the placenta and is concentrated in the fetal thyroid gland by 12 weeks’ gestation.

512
01:08:55,468 --> 01:09:05,093
It may cause severe or irreversible fetal and neonatal hypothyroidism, which can lead to decreased mental capacity and delayed skeletal maturation.

513
01:09:05,093 --> 01:09:16,080
Pregnancy testing should be performed before administration of radioiodine-131, and it is recommended that pregnancy be avoided for 6 to 12 months following treatment .

514
01:09:16,080 --> 01:09:29,468
A review of more than 3000 pregnancies in which women delayed conception for at least 6 months after receiving radioactive iodine treatment identified no increase in pregnancy losses or congenital malformations .

515
01:09:29,468 --> 01:09:34,893
Concerns for the breastfeeding mother stem from breast exposure to the radioisotope.

516
01:09:34,893 --> 01:09:36,993
# Warfarin.

517
01:09:36,993 --> 01:09:42,105
This anticoagulant is a vitamin K antagonist with a long half-life.

518
01:09:42,105 --> 01:09:50,043
Because of its low molecular weight, it readily crosses the placenta and may cause embryotoxic and fetotoxic effects.

519
01:09:50,043 --> 01:09:54,968
It is considered contraindicated in pregnancy with one important exception.

520
01:09:54,968 --> 01:10:04,993
As discussed in Chapter 52 (p. 922), warfarin is used to treat women with mechanical heart valves who are at high risk for thromboembolism .

521
01:10:04,993 --> 01:10:10,818
Warfarin embryopathy is characterized by stippled epiphyses and nasal hypoplasia .

522
01:10:10,818 --> 01:10:28,793
In one review of 63 cases attributed to warfarin exposure, 80 percent displayed characteristic findings, which include depressed nasal bridge with nasal hypoplasia and choanal atresia, along with stippled epiphyses of the femur, humerus, calcanei, and distal phalanges .

523
01:10:28,793 --> 01:10:34,380
The embryopathy may result from exposure between 6 and 9 weeks’ gestation .

524
01:10:34,380 --> 01:10:40,268
Prevalence following exposure during this critical period is estimated to be 6 percent.

525
01:10:40,268 --> 01:10:51,030
One metaanalysis of cases in which the warfarin dosage was ≤5 mg/d identified embryopathy in 1 percent of exposed fetuses.

526
01:10:51,030 --> 01:10:54,505
This suggests that risk may be dose dependent .

527
01:10:54,505 --> 01:10:56,868
Figure 8-4.

528
01:10:56,868 --> 01:11:07,793
Warfarin embryopathy or fetal warfarin syndrome: nasal hypoplasia and depressed nasal bridge seen in a fetal sonographic image (A) and in the same newborn (B).

529
01:11:07,793 --> 01:11:17,280
If used beyond the first trimester, warfarin may lead to hemorrhage into fetal structures, which can cause abnormal growth and deformation from scarring .

530
01:11:17,280 --> 01:11:25,118
Nearly 50 percent of reported embryopathy cases also have CNS anomalies (van Driel, 2002).

531
01:11:25,118 --> 01:11:36,430
Abnormalities can include agenesis of the corpus callosum; cerebellar vermian agenesis, which is the Dandy-Walker malformation; microphthalmia; and optic atrophy.

532
01:11:36,430 --> 01:11:38,030
Affected

533
01:11:38,188 --> 01:11:43,688
infants are also at risk for blindness, deafness, and developmental delays.

534
01:11:43,638 --> 01:11:45,838
# Tobacco.

535
01:11:45,838 --> 01:11:50,825
Cigarette smoking is the leading preventable cause of perinatal mortality.

536
01:11:50,825 --> 01:11:55,925
At least 7 percent of women report smoking cigarettes during pregnancy .

537
01:11:55,925 --> 01:12:06,950
Cigarette smoke contains a complex mixture of nicotine, cotinine, cyanide, thiocyanate, carbon monoxide, cadmium, lead, and various hydrocarbons .

538
01:12:06,950 --> 01:12:14,338
In addition to being fetotoxic, many of these substances have vasoactive effects or reduce oxygen levels.

539
01:12:14,338 --> 01:12:23,663
Although tobacco is not considered a major human teratogen, selected birth defects have been reported to occur with greater frequency following exposure.

540
01:12:23,663 --> 01:12:36,000
Cigarette smoking and exposure to second-hand smoke have been associated with up to a 1.5-fold risk for orofacial clefts (Kummet, 2016; Sabbagh, 2015).

541
01:12:36,000 --> 01:12:41,713
This equates to an attributable risk of approximately 1 case per 2000 births.

542
01:12:41,713 --> 01:12:49,813
It is plausible that the vasoactive properties of tobacco smoke could produce congenital defects related to vascular disturbances.

543
01:12:49,813 --> 01:13:03,925
For example, the prevalence of Poland sequence, which is caused by an interruption in the vascular supply to one side of the fetal chest and ipsilateral arm, has been reported to occur two times more commonly among cigarette smokers .

544
01:13:03,925 --> 01:13:15,050
The NBDPS similarly found an association between active or passive cigarette smoking and limb reduction defects, and an overall increase in risk of 25 percent .

545
01:13:15,050 --> 01:13:27,450
A small increased risk for fetal cardiac anomalies also has been reported and may be dose related (Alverson, 2011; Malik, 2008; Sullivan, 2015).

546
01:13:27,450 --> 01:13:33,888
The most prevalent obstetrical complication from smoking is a dosedependent reduction in fetal growth.

547
01:13:33,888 --> 01:13:41,813
Newborns of mothers who smoke weigh on average 200 g less than newborns of nonsmokers (D’Souza, 1981).

548
01:13:41,813 --> 01:13:48,550
Smoking doubles the risk of low birthweight and raises the risk of fetalgrowth restriction two- to threefold .

549
01:13:48,550 --> 01:13:54,200
As such, cigarette smoking accounts for 15 percent of low-birthweight neonates .

550
01:13:54,200 --> 01:13:58,463
Even secondhand smoke increases the risk for low birthweight.

551
01:13:58,463 --> 01:14:09,175
Other adverse pregnancy outcomes associated with cigarette smoking include preterm birth, prelabor rupture of membranes, placenta previa, and placenta abruption .

552
01:14:09,175 --> 01:14:19,550
Approximately one third of cases of sudden infant death syndrome have been attributed to cigarette smoking (Anderson, 2019; Dietz, 2010).

553
01:14:19,550 --> 01:14:23,725
Risks of childhood asthma and obesity also are increased.

554
01:14:23,725 --> 01:14:30,463
When inquiring about tobacco use, providers should include alternative nicotine delivery products.

555
01:14:30,463 --> 01:14:33,763
All forms of nicotine cross the placenta.

556
01:14:33,763 --> 01:14:36,800
None is considered safe in pregnancy.

557
01:14:36,800 --> 01:14:45,375
Vaping or e-cigarette products heat an “eliquid” containing nicotine—and often additional contaminants—along with a flavoring agent.

558
01:14:45,375 --> 01:14:57,250
Nicotine is associated with adverse effects on fetal brain and lung development (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020b; Spindel, 2016).

559
01:14:57,250 --> 01:15:05,100
In adults, vaping has been linked to serious lung injury known as EVALI—e-cigarette or vaping-associated lung injury .

560
01:15:05,100 --> 01:15:14,625
Animal studies of hookah use, in which tobacco smoke is filtered through a water bowl, demonstrate lower birthweight and increased markers of cardiac stress .

561
01:15:14,625 --> 01:15:18,138
Smoking cessation should be strongly encouraged.

562
01:15:18,138 --> 01:15:26,675
Counseling includes avoidance of secondhand smoke and all nicotine-replacement products, such as patches, gum, and lozenges.

563
01:15:26,675 --> 01:15:35,875
A hotline is available from the Centers of Disease Control and Prevention and can be accessed by calling 1-800-QUITNOW.

564
01:15:35,875 --> 01:15:44,775
The U.S. Preventive Services Task Force has recommended that behavioral interventions be offered at the initial visit and continued throughout pregnancy .

565
01:15:44,775 --> 01:15:47,775
# Herbal Medicinal Products.

566
01:15:47,775 --> 01:16:00,425
In large survey studies from North America and Europe, 30 to 60 percent of women report using herbal remedies during pregnancy (Kennedy, 2016; Pallivalapila, 2015).

567
01:16:00,425 --> 01:16:08,338
These products are not regulated by the FDA, and there is a paucity of controlled studies assessing their safety and efficacy.

568
01:16:08,338 --> 01:16:13,838
The identity, quantity, and purity of each ingredient are usually unknown.

569
01:16:13,838 --> 01:16:21,100
Authors of a recent systematic review that included more than 1 million pregnancies concluded that in the absence of more

570
01:16:21,268 --> 01:16:27,030
safety data, the use of herbal medicinal products in pregnancy should be discouraged .

571
01:16:26,980 --> 01:16:32,943
Selected herbal remedies and their reported adverse effects are shown in Table 8-5.

572
01:16:32,943 --> 01:16:35,280
TABLE 8-5.

573
01:16:35,280 --> 01:16:41,368
Pharmacological Actions and Adverse Effects of Some Herbal Medicines # Drugs of Abuse.

574
01:16:41,368 --> 01:16:45,030
Substance abuse is not uncommon in pregnancy.

575
01:16:45,030 --> 01:16:54,880
Assessment of outcomes attributable to illicit drug use is often confounded by factors such as poor maternal health, malnutrition, and infectious disease.

576
01:16:54,880 --> 01:17:01,118
Polysubstance abuse further complicates assessment of outcomes associated with any one drug.

577
01:17:01,118 --> 01:17:09,480
Moreover, illegal substances may contain toxic contaminants such as lead, cyanide, herbicides, and pesticides.

578
01:17:09,480 --> 01:17:15,568
Impurities added as diluents may independently have serious adverse perinatal effects.

579
01:17:15,568 --> 01:17:21,243
As noted on page 149, alcohol is a significant teratogen.

580
01:17:21,243 --> 01:17:28,255
Because it is legally obtained and ubiquitous, its use confounds the study of illicit drug teratogenicity.

581
01:17:28,255 --> 01:17:33,405
Similarly, tobacco use confounds the effect of drugs on fetal growth.

582
01:17:33,405 --> 01:17:40,955
Cocaine With this CNS stimulant, most adverse outcomes result from its vasoconstrictive and hypertensive effects.

583
01:17:40,955 --> 01:17:49,105
Serious potential maternal complications are cerebrovascular hemorrhage, myocardial damage, and placental abruption.

584
01:17:49,105 --> 01:18:09,043
Studies of congenital abnormalities and cocaine exposure have yielded conflicting results, but associations with cleft palate, cardiovascular abnormalities, and urinary tract anomalies have been reported (Chasnoff, 1988; Lipshultz, 1991; van Gelder, 2009).

585
01:18:09,043 --> 01:18:14,593
Cocaine use is also associated with fetal-growth restriction and preterm delivery.

586
01:18:14,593 --> 01:18:24,705
Children exposed as fetuses have risks for behavioral abnormalities and cognitive impairments (Bada, 2011; Gouin, 2011).

587
01:18:24,705 --> 01:18:29,868
Marijuana This is the recreational drug most commonly used in pregnancy .

588
01:18:29,868 --> 01:18:38,980
Based on data from the National Survey on Drug Use and Health, nearly 4 percent of pregnant women reported using marijuana in 2014 .

589
01:18:38,980 --> 01:18:49,693
Among women diagnosed with nausea and vomiting of pregnancy, the prevalence of marijuana use increased from 7 to 11 percent from 2009 to 2016 .

590
01:18:49,693 --> 01:18:55,418
Of concern is that the increase in prevalence is related to perceived safety and efficacy.

591
01:18:55,418 --> 01:19:05,968
In a study of 400 cannabis dispensaries, nearly 70 percent advised a caller posing as a pregnant woman with nausea at 8 weeks’ gestation to use marijuana .

592
01:19:05,968 --> 01:19:14,180
However, no data support efficacy of marijuana for nausea and vomiting of pregnancy, and there are indeed safety concerns .

593
01:19:14,180 --> 01:19:23,580
Cannabinoids are not considered to be major teratogens, but they cross the placenta, and endogenous cannabinoids play key roles in brain development.

594
01:19:23,580 --> 01:19:30,468
In animals, cannabinoids are involved in neuronal proliferation, migration, and differentiation .

595
01:19:30,468 --> 01:19:44,630
Adverse developmental outcomes reported in exposed children include decreased attention span and lower scores on tests of visual problem solving and visual-motor coordination (Fried, 2003; Willford 2010).

596
01:19:44,630 --> 01:19:51,005
Pregnancy outcome data are somewhat limited by the confounding risks of concomitant tobacco use.

597
01:19:51,005 --> 01:20:01,718
In one metaanalysis of nearly 8000 exposed pregnancies, only those with concomitant tobacco use had increased rates of preterm birth and low-birthweight neonates .

598
01:20:01,718 --> 01:20:13,643
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019b) has stated that the effects of marijuana may be as serious as those of cigarette smoking or alcohol consumption.

599
01:20:13,643 --> 01:20:21,105
Because of neurodevelopmental concerns, women who are pregnant or contemplating pregnancy should avoid marijuana use.

600
01:20:21,105 --> 01:20:26,368
Methamphetamine This sympathomimetic amine is derived from dextroamphetamine.

601
01:20:26,368 --> 01:20:30,393
It enhances dopamine release and blocks its reuptake.

602
01:20:30,393 --> 01:20:36,855
Methamphetamine is prescribed to treat attention deficit hyperactivity disorder and narcolepsy.

603
01:20:36,855 --> 01:20:41,543
Abuse has been rising in the United States since the late 1980s .

604
01:20:41,543 --> 01:20:45,280
Methamphetamine is not considered teratogenic.

605
01:20:45,280 --> 01:21:03,730
However, use in pregnancy is consistently associated with small-for-gestational age newborns, and children are at risk for developmental delays and behavioral abnormalities (Derauf, 2012; Eze, 2016; Gabrhelik, 2021; O’Connor, 2020).

606
01:21:03,730 --> 01:21:09,268
Behavioral abnormalities have been described in both infants and school-aged children .

607
01:21:09,436 --> 01:21:17,586
Hypertensive complications, placental abruption, preterm birth, and stillbirth are other associated adverse outcomes .

608
01:21:17,536 --> 01:21:25,423
Opioids The dramatic rise in narcotic use among nonpregnant and pregnant individuals has been aptly termed an epidemic.

609
01:21:25,423 --> 01:21:28,536
Opioids are not major teratogens.

610
01:21:28,536 --> 01:21:39,661
However, the NBDPS did identify a slightly greater risk for spina bifida, gastroschisis, and cardiac abnormalities with periconceptional opioid exposure .

611
01:21:39,661 --> 01:21:54,111
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019d) stresses that this potential, small increase in birth defects with maintenance therapy should be weighed against the risks associated with uncontrolled opioid abuse.

612
01:21:54,111 --> 01:22:02,548
Heroin addiction is associated with adverse pregnancy outcomes from the effects of repeated narcotic withdrawal on the fetus and placenta.

613
01:22:02,548 --> 01:22:09,011
These include preterm birth, placental abruption, fetal-growth restriction, and fetal death.

614
01:22:09,011 --> 01:22:25,786
Neonatal narcotic withdrawal, called the neonatal abstinence syndrome, may manifest in 40 to 90 percent of exposed newborns (Blinick, 1973; Creanga, 2012; Dashe, 2002; Zelson, 1973).

615
01:22:25,786 --> 01:22:39,348
As discussed in Chapter 33 (p. 605), CNS irritability may progress to seizures if untreated and may be accompanied by tachypnea, apneic episodes, poor feeding, and failure to thrive.

616
01:22:39,348 --> 01:22:46,773
At-risk neonates are closely monitored using a scoring system, and those severely affected are treated with opioids .

617
01:22:46,773 --> 01:22:58,098
The proportion of exposed newborns developing neonatal abstinence syndrome has risen significantly in recent years (Creanga, 2012; Lind, 2015).

618
01:22:58,098 --> 01:23:14,148
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019d) recommends that pregnant women with opioid-use disorder be maintained on opioid-agonist therapy to reduce the risks associated with illicit opioid abuse and associated behaviors.

619
01:23:14,148 --> 01:23:26,448
Treatment includes either buprenorphine, which may be given in an office-based setting by a licensed buprenorphine prescriber, or methadone, usually through a licensed outpatient opioid treatment program.

620
01:23:26,448 --> 01:23:34,373
A multidisciplinary treatment program is recommended to reduce the likelihood of additional opioid abuse while on maintenance therapy.

621
01:23:34,373 --> 01:23:39,823
Withdrawal from methadone during pregnancy is discouraged because of high relapse rates .

622
01:23:39,823 --> 01:23:49,036
At Parkland Hospital, pregnant opioid users who decline maintenance therapy are offered inpatient hospitalization for controlled methadone taper.

623
01:23:49,036 --> 01:23:57,348
The goal is to reduce the likelihood of neonatal abstinence syndrome (Dashe, 2002; Stewart, 2013).

624
01:23:57,348 --> 01:24:04,886
Miscellaneous Drugs Phencyclidine (PCP) or angel dust is not associated with congenital anomalies.

625
01:24:04,886 --> 01:24:13,811
More than half of exposed newborns, however, experience withdrawal symptoms characterized by tremors, jitteriness, and irritability.

626
01:24:13,811 --> 01:24:17,648
Toluene is a common solvent used in paints and glue.

627
01:24:17,648 --> 01:24:22,461
Occupational exposure is reported to have significant fetal risks .

628
01:24:22,461 --> 01:24:31,711
When abused by women in early pregnancy, it is associated with toluene embryopathy, which is phenotypically similar to fetal alcohol syndrome.

629
01:24:31,711 --> 01:24:41,711
Abnormalities include pre- and postnatal growth deficiency, microcephaly, midface hypoplasia, short palpebral fissures, and wide nasal bridge .

630
01:24:41,711 --> 01:24:46,236
Up to 40 percent of exposed children have developmental delays .

